# ghcacher_live.dl6 -- the LIVE conditional-fetch leg
# (plans/2026-08-04-ghcacher-plan.md sections 3.1 and 4.4).
#
# Transport is `gh api --include` with an explicit If-None-Match header. `gh`
# reads credentials from the OS keychain, so no token is ever interpolated into
# a template, an environment variable, or a tick log. `--cache` is NOT used: a
# TTL hit answers stale bytes without asking GitHub, and what this program needs
# is GitHub's own freshness confirmation.
#
# NO BACK-OFF, SLEEP, OR RETRY COLUMN EXISTS HERE, and none may be added: rate
# pressure is relational. A threshold rule that refuses to derive `poll` is a
# paused tick, and a paused tick derives nothing and costs nothing.

# ── the cadence ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The literal in `poll`'s body IS the schedule: emit_ts.pl collects exactly that
# literal and serve/2_binds.ts spins exactly that one rx interval, whose first
# emission is one period after boot. 2 is what keeps the live smoke inside the
# 10-second law; the design cadence for a 300-repo poller is 60.
#
# rx: interval(2_000).pipe(map(toBucketRow), mergeMap(rows => engine.submit(rows)))
use http.

rel interval(period: int, bucket: int).

# The world names an endpoint. EDB: a bare top-level fact is refused
# (level_rule_no_positive_body), so this arrives over /arrivals.
#
# rx: arrivals$.pipe(filter(row => row.rel === 'watch_endpoint'))
rel watch_endpoint(endpoint_path: text).

# ── the clock latch ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TWO rules and not one, and the split is forced from both sides. The cadence
# has to be the LITERAL argument 1 of an `interval` atom, because emit_ts.pl
# bind_read_literals/4 spins one rx timer per literal it finds there and a
# variable emits a bind plan with no timer at all. A literal in an edge rule's
# TRIGGER atom is refused (trigger_arg_not_var). So the literal read is a level
# rule and the latch is the edge rule above it.
#
# rx: intervalFired$.pipe(map(toBucketRow))
rel bucket_event(period: int, bucket: int).
bucket_event(2, Bucket) <- interval(2, Bucket).

# key(1) keys on `period`, so a new bucket REPLACES rather than accumulates.
# That replacement is what bounds the poll: `interval` rows themselves never
# depart, so a rule reading them directly would re-demand every bucket it has
# ever seen. A keyed head must be an edge head (keyed_level_head refuses the
# level form).
#
# rx: bucketEvent$.pipe(scan((latch, row) => latch.set(row.period, row), new Map()))
rel current_bucket(period: int, bucket: int) key(1).
current_bucket(Period, Bucket) <+ bucket_event(Period, Bucket).

# ── the conditional request ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `bucket` is FRESHNESS (registry.pl host_input_contract/3): it never appears in
# the template, it extends the witness, so a new bucket re-asks the same
# question and an unchanged resource absorbs as zero delta. `prev_etag` is
# IDENTITY, so it returns on the response row and a new tag is a new question.
#
# `|| true` is not defensive: MEASURED 2026-08-04 against gh 2.92.0, a 304 exits
# 1 with `gh: HTTP 304` on stderr, and a nonzero exit is a host failure at this
# seam (serve/1_hosts.ts runShellLine). The status is a VALUE here.
#
# One compact JSON object is the answer shape, not four printf lines: on a 304
# the body is empty, which collapses a per-column read to three lines against
# four declared columns and lands in the whitespace-grid reading instead
# (serve/1_hosts.ts parseWhitespace). A named projection has no such ambiguity.
#
# `grep -i '^etag:'` is anchored because Access-Control-Expose-Headers lists
# both ETag and X-RateLimit-Remaining by name in its value.
rel get(url: text, headers: text, prev_etag: text, bucket: int)
  -> (status: int, response_headers: json, body: json, bytes: int).

# The conditional GET is the PROGRAM's: If-None-Match is a header row, so the
# transport carries no ETag and no cache of its own.
rel request_header(endpoint_path: text, header_name: text, header_value: text).
rel request_headers(endpoint_path: text, headers: text).
request_header(EndpointPath, "Accept", "application/vnd.github+json") <-
  watch_endpoint(EndpointPath).
request_header(EndpointPath, "If-None-Match", PrevEtag) <-
  poll_state(EndpointPath, PrevEtag),
  PrevEtag != ''.
request_headers(EndpointPath, json_object(HeaderName, HeaderValue)) <-
  request_header(EndpointPath, HeaderName, HeaderValue).

# ── the etag latch ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `etag_event` is the DELAY that makes the poll loop legal, and it is not
# decoration. The loop poll_state -> poll -> rest_call -> fresh_etag ->
# poll_state closes through a trigger whose source is LEVEL-headed, which is
# grade 0, so the whole cycle sums to 0 and the clock checker refuses it
# (unconstructive_clock_cycle, MEASURED on the direct spelling that section 4.4
# writes). A trigger read off an EDGE-headed rel is grade 1
# (3_clock_check.pl edge_goal_dependency), so this one hop makes the cycle
# productive_delayed. It is also exactly the rel the 304 golden pins, one tick
# apart from the response it now derives from rather than being fed.
#
# rx: freshEtag$.pipe(delay(0))   the one-tick hop
rel etag_event(endpoint_path: text, etag_value: text) log keep(all).
etag_event(EndpointPath, NextEtag) <+ fresh_etag(EndpointPath, NextEtag).

# Two edge arms into one key(1) head: the bootstrap arm gives the first poll an
# empty If-None-Match, the refresh arm replaces it. Only a 200 moves the latch,
# which is why the 304's WEAKNESS-STRIPPED tag never lands (MEASURED: a 200
# answers `W/"abc"`, its own 304 answers `"abc"`).
#
# rx: merge(watchEndpoint$.pipe(map(row => [row.path, ''])),
#           etagEvent$).pipe(scan((latch, [path, tag]) => latch.set(path, tag), new Map()))
rel poll_state(endpoint_path: text, etag_value: text) key(1).
poll_state(EndpointPath, '') <+ watch_endpoint(EndpointPath).
poll_state(EndpointPath, EtagValue) <+ etag_event(EndpointPath, EtagValue).

# ── the poll ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The witness is (endpoint_path, prev_etag, bucket). Two things move it: a new
# bucket, and a new tag. So the first 200 re-derives this rule with the tag it
# just learned, inside the same bucket, and that second call is the one that
# answers 304.
#
# rx: combineLatest([watchEndpoint$, pollState$, currentBucket$]).pipe(map(toPollRows))
rel poll(endpoint_path: text, prev_etag: text, bucket: int).
poll(EndpointPath, PrevEtag, Bucket) <-
  watch_endpoint(EndpointPath),
  poll_state(EndpointPath, PrevEtag),
  current_bucket(2, Bucket).

# rx: poll$.pipe(map(mintIdentityAndWitness), distinct(row => row.witnessDigest),
#                mergeMap(runShell), mergeMap(commitEdbArrival))
rel rest_arrival(endpoint_path: text, bucket: int, status_code: int,
                 response_headers: json, body_json: json).
rest_arrival(EndpointPath, Bucket, StatusCode, RespHeaders, BodyJson) <-
  poll(EndpointPath, PrevEtag, Bucket),
  request_headers(EndpointPath, Headers),
  get(EndpointPath, Headers, PrevEtag, Bucket, StatusCode, RespHeaders,
           BodyJson, _Bytes).

rel header_etag(endpoint_path: text, bucket: int, etag_value: text).
header_etag(EndpointPath, Bucket, EtagValue) <-
  rest_arrival(EndpointPath, Bucket, _StatusCode, RespHeaders, _BodyJson),
  decode(RespHeaders, {etag: EtagValue: text}).

rel header_rest_remaining(endpoint_path: text, bucket: int, remaining: int).
header_rest_remaining(EndpointPath, Bucket, Remaining) <-
  rest_arrival(EndpointPath, Bucket, _StatusCode, RespHeaders, _BodyJson),
  decode(RespHeaders, {"x-ratelimit-remaining": Remaining: int}).

rel rest_call(endpoint_path: text, bucket: int, status_code: int,
              next_etag: text, body_json: json, rest_remaining: int).
rest_call(EndpointPath, Bucket, StatusCode, NextEtag, BodyJson, RestRemaining) <-
  rest_arrival(EndpointPath, Bucket, StatusCode, _RespHeaders, BodyJson),
  coalesce(header_etag(EndpointPath, Bucket, NextEtag), ''),
  coalesce(header_rest_remaining(EndpointPath, Bucket, RestRemaining), -1).

# ── what a 200 buys ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# rx: restCall$.pipe(filter(row => row.statusCode === 200), map(toEtagPair))
rel fresh_etag(endpoint_path: text, etag_value: text).
fresh_etag(EndpointPath, NextEtag) <-
  rest_call(EndpointPath, _Bucket, StatusCode, NextEtag, _BodyJson, _RestRemaining),
  StatusCode == 200.

# rx: restCall$.pipe(filter(row => row.statusCode === 200), map(toBodyPair))
rel fresh_body(endpoint_path: text, body_json: json).
fresh_body(EndpointPath, BodyJson) <-
  rest_call(EndpointPath, _Bucket, StatusCode, _NextEtag, BodyJson, _RestRemaining),
  StatusCode == 200.

# THE CACHE. A LATCH, never a level rule: a level rule loses the body the moment
# its poll row is superseded, which is the defect section 1.1 measured. A 304
# leaves this table byte-identical, and that is the whole receipt.
#
# rx: freshBody$.pipe(scan((cache, row) => cache.set(row.path, row.body), new Map()))
rel cached_body(endpoint_path: text, body_json: json) key(1).
cached_body(EndpointPath, BodyJson) <+ fresh_body(EndpointPath, BodyJson).

# ── the rate pool ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Keyed by endpoint rather than as a single global row: key(N) keys on the FIRST
# N columns, so a one-column keyed rel keys on its own value and accumulates
# instead of replacing.
#
# rx: restCall$.pipe(scan((pool, row) => pool.set(row.path, row.remaining), new Map()))
rel rest_pool(endpoint_path: text, remaining_points: int) key(1).
rest_pool(EndpointPath, RestRemaining) <+
  rest_call(EndpointPath, _Bucket, _StatusCode, _NextEtag, _BodyJson, RestRemaining).

# ── telemetry ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Two arms with a literal each, because the language has no `if`. Edge arms, not
# level arms: a level `call_log` would lose every row whose `rest_call` was
# superseded by the next poll, and an audit trail that retracts is not one.
#
# rx: merge(restCall$.pipe(filter(row => row.statusCode === 304), map(withHit(1))),
#           restCall$.pipe(filter(row => row.statusCode !== 304), map(withHit(0))))
rel call_log(endpoint_path: text, bucket: int, status_code: int,
             cache_hit: int, remaining_points: int) log keep(all).
call_log(EndpointPath, Bucket, StatusCode, 1, RestRemaining) <+
  rest_call(EndpointPath, Bucket, StatusCode, _NextEtag, _BodyJson, RestRemaining),
  StatusCode == 304.
call_log(EndpointPath, Bucket, StatusCode, 0, RestRemaining) <+
  rest_call(EndpointPath, Bucket, StatusCode, _NextEtag, _BodyJson, RestRemaining),
  StatusCode \== 304.

? cached_body(EndpointPath, BodyJson).
? call_log(EndpointPath, Bucket, StatusCode, CacheHit, RemainingPoints).
